          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been diagnosed with high blood pressure.",
          "answer": "A diagnosis of high blood pressure means the pressure in your arteries stays raised over time. Blood pressure is written as two numbers: the systolic (when the heart beats) over the diastolic (between beats). A diagnosis rests on repeated readings, not one. Pressure naturally rises and falls through the day, so a single high reading is not a diagnosis by itself. Most people with it have no symptoms, which is why it is usually found during routine checks. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nFor adults, US society guidance classifies office readings like this (source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*):\n\n- **Normal:** top number below 120 and bottom number below 80.\n- **Elevated:** top number 120 to 129, bottom number below 80.\n- **Stage 1 hypertension:** top number 130 to 139, or bottom number 80 to 89.\n- **Stage 2 hypertension:** top number 140 or higher, or bottom number 90 or higher.\n\nIf the two numbers fall into different categories, the higher one applies. Other guidance uses different thresholds, so your clinician's classification may differ from this one.\n\nCommon lifestyle steps described in the corpus include:\n\n- **Regular aerobic activity:** the usual target is 150 minutes a week of moderate activity, such as brisk walking or cycling, plus muscle-strengthening on two or more days. (Source: *Physical activity for high blood pressure*.)\n- **Weight reduction:** lowering weight tends to lower blood pressure in proportion to the amount lost, and even modest loss helps. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*.)\n- **Limiting alcohol:** drinking well above recommended limits raises blood pressure, and cutting back is one of the more reliable non-drug steps. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*.)\n- **A whole-pattern diet:** single \"superfood\" or supplement remedies do not have comparable evidence. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*.)\n\nOn the diagnosis code: the US ICD-10-CM code for essential (primary) hypertension is I10. It is a complete code with no further characters needed. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10*.) I could not check the coding lookup tool in this session, so that is all I can say on the code.\n\nI can't tell you whether your own readings or treatment are right for you. Your clinician can explain your numbers, your target, and which options suit you. If you have chest pain, sudden severe headache, fainting, weakness on one side, or trouble speaking, seek emergency care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
