          "trace_id": "c444e57fb39e4536ad192ce32969b251",
          "session_id": "full-mt-m-001",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What about diet?",
          "answer": "Before going further, I need to know what you mean by diet. Are you asking about diet for high blood pressure, another condition, a symptom such as reflux, or a general goal such as healthy eating? Your answer will change what is useful to say.\n\nHere is what the corpus says about diet for high blood pressure, since that was the topic you raised earlier:\n\n- **Eat a whole pattern, not a single \"blood pressure food.\"** The approach with the most evidence emphasizes vegetables, fruit, whole grains, low-fat dairy, poultry, fish, beans, nuts and vegetable oils. It limits saturated fat, full-fat dairy, fatty meat, sweets and sugar-sweetened drinks. Swapping one food into an otherwise unchanged diet does not give the same result. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*.)\n- **Reduce sodium (salt).** Cutting sodium adds to the effect of the pattern above, and the combination works better than either alone. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*.) Much of the sodium most people eat comes from packaged and restaurant food, not the salt shaker. (Source: *Diet and fluid in heart failure*, which notes this point.)\n\nThe corpus does not give a single diet that suits everyone. Its general diabetes guidance says several eating patterns have support, and the one a person can keep up with is the deciding factor. (Source: *Diet for type 2 diabetes*.)\n\nFor advice fitted to your situation, including any medicines you take or other health conditions, please talk with your clinician or a registered dietitian.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
